1
00:00:02,967 --> 00:00:05,117
Look again at the altitude.

2
00:00:05,117 --> 00:00:07,630
It cuts the base into two pieces.

3
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:14,127
The blue piece is the shadow of side c on the base, its projection: c cos B.

4
00:00:14,127 --> 00:00:18,319
The pink piece is the projection of b: b cos C.

5
00:00:18,534 --> 00:00:21,023
Together they make the whole base.

6
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:24,760
So a equals b cos C plus c cos B.

7
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:30,020
Each side is the sum of the other two, each times the cosine of the angle next to it.

8
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:35,178
If an angle is obtuse, its cosine is negative and the formula still holds.

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,822
Turn everything into sides.

10
00:00:38,034 --> 00:00:40,796
Replace both cosines by the cosine law.

11
00:00:40,796 --> 00:00:43,017
The b and the c cancel neatly.

12
00:00:43,234 --> 00:00:48,111
Subtract: two b squared minus two c squared over two a.

13
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:50,224
Multiply by a. Done.

14
00:00:50,224 --> 00:00:55,288
With thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, both sides are minus twenty nine.

15
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,874
This time, turn everything into sines.

16
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,200
Two sin cos is sin of the double angle.

17
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:09,751
Sum to product; and B plus C is one eighty minus A, so its sine is sin A.

18
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:11,474
Two R sin A is a.

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,469
Exam tip.

20
00:01:15,469 --> 00:01:21,263
In an identity, turn everything into sides, or everything into sines.

21
00:01:21,263 --> 00:01:22,282
Never leave a mixture.

22
00:01:27,634 --> 00:01:30,839
Areas and radii need the half angles.

23
00:01:30,839 --> 00:01:37,727
Start from one minus cos A, which is two sin squared of half A, and put in the cosine law.

24
00:01:37,727 --> 00:01:44,472
The top is a difference of two squares: a plus b minus c, times a minus b plus c.

25
00:01:44,472 --> 00:01:46,233
Each is twice s minus something.

26
00:01:46,434 --> 00:01:57,783
So sin of half A is the square root of s minus b, times s minus c, over b c. Positive, because half A is less than ninety degrees.

27
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,298
One plus cos A gives the cosine: root of s times s minus a, over b c.

28
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:14,822
And dividing, the b c cancels: tan of half A is root of s minus b, s minus c, over s, s minus a.

29
00:02:16,034 --> 00:02:20,580
The commonest slip in this unit: s is half the perimeter.

30
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:27,372
Check every time that s minus a, s minus b and s minus c add up to s.

31
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,856
The running triangle again.

32
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:35,524
s is twenty one, and the three differences are eight, seven and six.

33
00:02:35,524 --> 00:02:37,087
They add to twenty one.

34
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:37,390
Good.

35
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:43,294
Sin of half A is root a fifth; cos of half A is root four fifths.

36
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:46,588
Tan of half A is exactly a half.

37
00:02:46,588 --> 00:02:50,893
So A is fifty three degrees eight minutes, as the cosine law found.

38
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:54,760
Three sides, three angles.

39
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:56,466
s is seven and a half.

40
00:02:56,667 --> 00:03:02,983
Tan of half A is point one nine two five: A is twenty one degrees forty seven minutes.

41
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:09,803
Tan of half C is root three, so half C is sixty and C is one hundred and twenty.

42
00:03:09,803 --> 00:03:12,026
Obtuse, facing the longest side.

43
00:03:12,234 --> 00:03:14,978
B is what is left: thirty eight thirteen.

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,486
Which law for which data?

45
00:03:18,486 --> 00:03:21,767
Two angles and a side: the sine law.

46
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:29,580
Two sides and an opposite angle: also the sine law, but watch for two possible triangles, which is the next unit.

47
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:34,254
Two sides and the included angle: cosine or tangent law.

48
00:03:34,254 --> 00:03:37,317
Three sides: cosine or half angle laws.

49
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:48,180
The area is half base times height, and the height from A is b sin C.

50
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:51,057
So the area is half a b sin C.

51
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:56,898
Write sin A as two sin half A cos half A, and use the half angle laws.

52
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:07,622
The b c cancels, and the area is the square root of s, s minus a, s minus b, s minus c: Heron's formula.

53
00:04:07,622 --> 00:04:09,453
Area from the three sides alone.

54
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:15,372
For our triangle: root of twenty one times eight times seven times six.

55
00:04:15,372 --> 00:04:16,689
Exactly eighty four.

56
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:21,560
Every triangle has three kinds of special circle.

57
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:28,516
The circumcircle passes through the three vertices; its radius R is a b c over four delta.

58
00:04:28,734 --> 00:04:33,280
The incircle sits inside, touching all three sides.

59
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:35,995
Its radius r is delta over s.

60
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:43,350
And an excircle sits outside, touching side a and the other two sides extended.

61
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:47,191
Its radius r one is delta over s minus a.

62
00:04:47,191 --> 00:04:50,835
There are three of these, one opposite each vertex.

63
00:04:51,067 --> 00:04:58,189
For our triangle: R is eight point one two five, r is exactly four, and r one is ten and a half.

64
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:00,370
All five radii.

65
00:05:00,634 --> 00:05:07,102
Collect the numbers: area eighty four, s twenty one, and eight, seven, six.

66
00:05:07,334 --> 00:05:12,791
R is twenty seven thirty over three thirty six: eight point one two five.

67
00:05:12,791 --> 00:05:15,565
r is eighty four over twenty one: four.

68
00:05:15,767 --> 00:05:19,398
The exradii: ten and a half, twelve and fourteen.

69
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:22,306
A favourite proof.

70
00:05:22,634 --> 00:05:26,007
Write every radius as delta over something.

71
00:05:26,234 --> 00:05:33,891
Pair them cleverly: s minus a plus s minus b is c, and s minus s minus c is also c.

72
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:37,135
Add, and the top simplifies to a b.

73
00:05:37,135 --> 00:05:39,447
The bottom is delta squared, by Heron.

74
00:05:39,667 --> 00:05:44,525
So the total is a b c over delta, which is four R.

75
00:05:44,525 --> 00:05:47,283
And our numbers agree: thirty two and a half.

76
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:51,673
Pause and think.

77
00:05:51,673 --> 00:05:52,943
The sine law ratio.

78
00:05:59,834 --> 00:06:02,689
Two R, the diameter of the circumcircle.

79
00:06:04,667 --> 00:06:05,580
One more.

80
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:08,666
Area eighty four, semi perimeter twenty one.

81
00:06:15,534 --> 00:06:20,067
Four: delta over s, eighty four over twenty one.

82
00:06:20,067 --> 00:06:23,075
Eight point one two five is the circumradius.

83
00:06:25,267 --> 00:06:26,323
Quick recap.

84
00:06:26,323 --> 00:06:29,722
The sine law, with its common ratio two R.

85
00:06:29,722 --> 00:06:33,185
The cosine law, Pythagoras with a correction.

86
00:06:33,185 --> 00:06:35,555
The tangent and projection laws.

87
00:06:35,555 --> 00:06:39,188
The half angle laws in terms of the semi perimeter.

88
00:06:39,188 --> 00:06:45,418
And the area, by Heron, with the circumradius, inradius and exradii.

89
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,802
That was the properties of a triangle.

90
00:06:51,802 --> 00:07:00,839
Next, solution of triangles: using these laws to find every missing side and angle, including the ambiguous case.

91
00:07:00,839 --> 00:07:01,384
See you there.

